Read a Logical File (RPG) - INSCLMSLF, keyed DESCEND on CLMNO
====================================================================

This reads INSCLMSLF, the real logical file we worked through in
DDS/LOGICAL_FILE.DDS's "Runtime gotchas" / CPD7948 / CPD7486
troubleshooting (record format INSCLMSREC, built over physical file
INSCLMS, keyed DESCEND on CLMNO, omitting any claim where ST = 'NY').

Because the sort and the filter both live in the DDS, this program
needs zero sorting logic and zero "skip NY rows" logic - by the time
RPG sees a record, both are already done.


Declare the file
--------------------

     FINSCLMSLF IF   E           K DISK

Meaning:
	• INSCLMSLF - the logical file name (not the physical INSCLMS)
	• I = input only, F = full procedural, E = externally described
	  (field attributes come straight from the DDS - no D-specs needed
	  for CLMNO, CUSTOMER, STREET, CITY, ST, or NOTES below)
	• K = keyed - reads follow the LF's own access path (CLMNO
	  DESCEND), so the highest claim number comes back first


Read every visible record, highest CLMNO first
----------------------------------------------------

     FINSCLMSLF IF   E           K DISK

     C                   READ      INSCLMSLF
     C                   DOW       NOT %EOF(INSCLMSLF)

     C                   DSPLY     CLMNO
     C                   DSPLY     CUSTOMER
     C                   DSPLY     STREET

     C                   READ      INSCLMSLF

     C                   ENDDO

     C                   SETON                                        LR

Meaning:
	• READ INSCLMSLF reads the NEXT record in the file's own order;
	  since the access path is CLMNO DESCEND, that's highest CLMNO to
	  lowest, and any ST = 'NY' row never shows up here at all
	• %EOF(INSCLMSLF) turns on once the last visible record has been
	  read - the standard READ / DOW / ENDDO loop shape
	• CLMNO, CUSTOMER, STREET are usable with no D-specs at all,
	  because "E" on the F-spec pulls every field's name/length/type
	  straight from INSCLMSLF's DDS


Jump straight to the most recent claim (highest CLMNO)
------------------------------------------------------------

     FINSCLMSLF IF   E           K DISK

     C                   SETLL     *LOVAL        INSCLMSLF
     C                   READ      INSCLMSLF

     C                   IF        NOT %EOF(INSCLMSLF)
     C                   DSPLY     CLMNO
     C                   DSPLY     CUSTOMER
     C                   ENDIF

     C                   SETON                                        LR

Meaning:
	• The access path is DESCEND, so *LOVAL is actually the HIGHEST
	  key value in this file's read order - positioning to *LOVAL and
	  reading once hands back the single most recent claim, with no
	  sorting written in RPG at all
	• This is the practical payoff of building the key descending in
	  the DDS instead of ascending and reading the file backwards


Quick reference
-------------------
	• IF E ... K DISK -> input-only, externally described, keyed file
	• READ filename     -> reads the next record in access-path order
	• %EOF(filename)     -> true once the last record has been read
	• SETLL *LOVAL       -> positions before the first record in key
	  order (the HIGHEST value, on a DESCEND key)
	• Fields with no D-spec at all - "E" on the F-spec means RPG
	  already knows their name/type/length from the DDS
